  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+2X+5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+2X+5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+2A+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+2A+5I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14+6+5 & 5+10+0 \\\\ 1+2+0 & 9+(-4)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 15 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
